Calculate the entropy change (J/mol K) of the given reaction. The molar entropies [J/K mol] are given in brackets after each substance.

\[ 2 \text{PbS(s)} [91.2] + 3 \text{O}_2(g) [205.1] \rightarrow 2 \text{PbO(s)} [66.5] + 2 \text{SO}_2(g) [248.2] \]<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The change in entropy (ΔS) for a reaction can be calculated using the following formula:

ΔS = Σ(n_f * C_T(F)) - Σ(n_c * C_T(C))

where:
*  n = stoichiometric coefficient in the balanced equation
*  C_T = molar heat capacity (which is equal to molar entropy in this case)
*  f = products
*  c = reactants

In this given reaction:

2  PbS(s)  + 3  O2(g)  → 2  PbO(s)  + 2  SO2(g)

Given molar entropes:
*  C_T(PbS) = 91.2 J/K mol
*  C_T(O2) = 205.1  G/K mol
*  C_T(PbO) = 66.5  G/K mol
*  C_T(SO2) = 248.2  G/K mol

Now, let plug the values into the formula:

ΔS = (2 * 66.5) + (2 * 248.2) - (2 * 91.2) - (3 * 205.1)
   = 133 + 496 - 182 - 616
   = 629 - 796
   = -167.0  mol K

Therefore, the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction is -167.0 J/mol K.

**Final answer:** -167.0
