","We to evaluate the definite integral
$$ \int_{-1}^{2} \left[(x^{2} + 3) - 1\right] \, dx $$
First, we simplify the expression inside the integral:
$$ \int_{-1}^{2} (x^{2} + 3 - 1) \, dx = \int_{-1}^{2} (x^{2} + 2) \, dx $$
Now, we integrate the expression:
$$ \int (x^{2} + 2) \, dx = \frac{x^{3}}{3} + 2x + C $$
Now, we evaluate the definite integral:
$$ \int_{-1}^{2} (x^{2}+2) \, dx = \left[ \frac{x^{3}}{3} + 2x \right] \Big{-1}^{2} = \left( \frac{2^{3}}{3} + 2(2) \right) - \left( \frac{(-1)^{3}}{3} + 2(-1) \right) $$
$$ = \left( \frac{8}{3} + 4 \right) - \left( -\frac{1}{3} - 2 \right) = \frac{8}{3} + 4 + \frac{1}{3} + 2 = \frac{8+1}{3} + 4+2 = \frac{9}{3} + 6 = 3 + 6 = 9 $$

Thus,
$$ \int_{-1}^{2} \left[(x^{2} + 3) - 1\right] \, dx = 9 $$

