","Let Let the distance from the starting point when b cycles is x km.
Let the time taken by a to reach the distance x is t hours.
Since a starts at a speed of 10 kmph, the distance covered by a is given as:
$10t = x$
Since b starts 3 hours after a, the time taken by b to reach the same distance x is $(t-3)$ hours.
Since b cycles at a speed of 20 kms, the distance covered by b is given as:
$20(t-3) = x$
Now, we have two equation:
$10t = x$
$20(t-3) = x$
Since both equal to x, we can equate the two equations:
$10t = 20(t-3)$
$10t = 20t - 60$
$10t = 60$
$t = 6$
Now, we can find the distance x using the first equation:
$x = 110t = 10(6) = 60$
So, the distance from the start when b cycles is 60 km.
The time taken by a to reach 60 km is 60/10 = 6 hours.
The time taken by b to reach 60 km is (6-3) = 3 hours.
The distance of the meeting is 60 km.

